  "title": "BNCI 2015-009 AMUSE (Auditory Multi-class Spatial ERP) dataset",
  "description": "EEG recordings from 21 participants performing an offline auditory multi-class spatial oddball (AMUSE) task for a P300-based brain-computer interface. Five loudspeakers in the frontal plane presented unique auditory stimuli, and spatial location served as the discriminating cue. Three conditions varied the inter-stimulus interval and speaker configuration. The data are 60-channel EEG with EOG, distributed in preprocessed form.",
  "methods_description": "EEG was recorded with a Brain Products amplifier (60 EEG and 2 EOG channels, nose reference, 1000 Hz raw, 0.1-250 Hz analog bandpass). Participants attended to target stimuli from one of five speakers, either by mentally counting or by keypress. Data were low-pass filtered, downsampled to 100 Hz and epoched, and epochs with ocular deflections above 70 µV were rejected.",
